# nomo snapshot v1
# worksheet: nonfinite

=== text ===
# Non-finite results
NaN, the infinities, and signed zero.

These are in the corpus for the cross-target comparison rather than for the
reader. NaN is the one value WebAssembly does not fully pin: the payload bits,
and the sign of a NaN produced from non-NaN operands, are both left to the
implementation. So a NaN computed here and a NaN computed in a browser may
differ in bits while being equally correct, and if those bits ever reached a
snapshot the golden suite would fail for a reason that is not a bug.

Every route to a NaN the language offers is therefore exercised, so that the
native and WebAssembly snapshots have to agree on all of them.
Undefined quotients and products.
zero_over_zero = 0/0 = NaN
zero_times_infinity = 0·∞ = NaN
Out of domain. Nomo has no complex numbers, so these are NaN and not errors.
root_of_negative = sqrt(0 - 1) = NaN
log_of_negative = ln(0 - 1) = NaN
arcsine_out_of_range = asin(2) = NaN
The indeterminate difference.
infinity_less_infinity = ∞ - ∞ = NaN
The infinities themselves are exactly representable and carry units.
positive = 1/0 = ∞
negative = 0 - 1/0 = -∞
with_units = 1 m/0 = ∞ m
Signed zero is a real IEEE 754 distinction and a deterministic one, unlike a
NaN payload, so it is pinned rather than normalised away. Note that `0 - 0.0`
is *positive* zero: only unary negation reaches the other one.
negative_zero = -0
positive_zero = 0 - 0 = 0
sum_of_zeros = 0 + negative_zero = 0 + 0 = 0
The difference is observable, which is why it is worth pinning.
reciprocal_of_negative_zero = 1/negative_zero = 1/0 = -∞

