<p class="prose">NaN, the infinities, and signed zero.</p>
<p class="prose">These are in the corpus for the cross-target comparison rather than for the reader. NaN is the one value WebAssembly does not fully pin: the payload bits, and the sign of a NaN produced from non-NaN operands, are both left to the implementation. So a NaN computed here and a NaN computed in a browser may differ in bits while being equally correct, and if those bits ever reached a snapshot the golden suite would fail for a reason that is not a bug.</p>
<p class="prose">Every route to a NaN the language offers is therefore exercised, so that the native and WebAssembly snapshots have to agree on all of them.</p>
